cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "The GEO Handbook"
message: "If you use this handbook, please cite it."
type: dataset
authors:
  - family-names: "Aporta Franco"
    given-names: "Fernando"
    alias: ferinazumaDEV
abstract: >-
  A comprehensive, primary-sourced reference on Generative Engine Optimization
  (GEO): structuring, writing, and publishing content so it is understood,
  trusted, and cited by generative AI answer engines.
repository-code: "https://github.com/ferinazumaDEV/generative-engine-optimization-handbook"
url: "https://zentimes.es"
# Concept DOI: resolves to the latest archived release, so it does not change when a new
# version is tagged. Version DOIs live on the Zenodo record page, not here.
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.22299644"
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: "10.5281/zenodo.22299644"
    description: "Concept DOI — always the latest version"
# ONE license id, deliberately, and it must stay a single value. This file is what
# Zenodo reads to archive a release, and its deposit format takes a single license id:
# a YAML list here converts to {"id": ["A", "B"]}, Zenodo rejects the deposit, and the
# release is archived as Failed with no DOI minted. That is what happened to the
# cookbook's v0.1.0 on 4 Sep 2026, reproduced with cffconvert 2.0.0. Keep this equal to
# the root LICENSE, which is also what GitHub's own detection reports. If this repository
# ever becomes multi-licensed, state that in README.md and LICENSES/, never here.
license: CC-BY-SA-4.0
# version and date-released must be bumped together with the git tag; the tag is the
# source of truth, these two are the copy CFF requires.
version: "0.1.1"
date-released: "2026-09-04"
keywords:
  - generative engine optimization
  - GEO
  - AI search
  - answer engines
  - LLM citation
